DOTPLAN(1)                USER COMMANDS                DOTPLAN(1)NAME     dotplan - Generate interesting .plan filesSYNOPSIS     dotplan [ style | s | d ] textDESCRIPTION     dotplan takes the supplied text (optionally  between  quotes     to  reduce  the  possibility  of conflict with a shell meta-     charater) and displays it in one of many ways.  The  results     can be redirected (via '>') to the user's .plan file.     The meanings of the options are:     style tells dotplan to display the given text in one of  its           many styles.     s     displays the list of styles, using each style  on  its           description.   It's nice to be able to compare various           styles and it helps when the user can't think of  any-           thing to display.     d     displays the given text in all styles, one at a time.EXAMPLES     dotplan 3 This is sample text # Display string using style 3     dotplan                       # Display usage information     dotplan s                     # Display styles in all styles     dotplay d This is more text   # Display string in all stylesAUTHORS     Roger Murray <rem@remsit.UUCP>     Marc Kriguer <kriguer@ernie.bekeley.edu>DIAGNOSTICS     "Huh? (bad style number)" means the user has tried to select     a  style  that doesn't exist.  "Huh? (bad letter)" means the     user has chosen an option that doesn't exist.BUGS     The resulting .plan files can look  pretty  nice.   However,     people  using  high  speed  terminals  (terminals on cluster     lines, Xenix consoles,  etc.)  won't  really  get  the  full     effect  when viewing them.  I suppose there are worse things     in the world.SEE ALSO     finger(1) rn(1) warp(6)Sun Release 3.2           Last change:                          1